course: language
lesson: 15
title: The apostrophe war
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The apostrophe (') has two jobs: it shows missing letters (it's = it is, don't = do not) and it shows belonging (the dog's bowl); it never makes a plain plural, so "Apple's 40p" on a shop sign is wrong.
explain: Where it sits tells you how many owners there are: the dog's bowl (one dog), the dogs' bowl (several dogs sharing), the dog's bowls (one dog, lots of bowls).
explain: Plurals that don't end in s just add 's: the children's toys, the men's toilets, the people's choice; plurals ending in s add only ': the ladies' toilets.
explain: Grown-ups really do fight about it: Birmingham took apostrophes off its street signs in 2009, the bookshop Waterstones dropped its apostrophe in 2012, and an Apostrophe Protection Society ran from 2001 to 2019.
explain: Some questions have two accepted answers: James's book and James' book are both used, and even famous places disagree: St James' Park in Newcastle, St James's Park in London; pick one style and stick to it, and copy official names exactly.
fact: An apostrophe shows missing letters or shows belonging; it is not used to make plurals.
fact: The greengrocer's apostrophe is the name for a wrong apostrophe in a plural, like "Banana's 30p"; a greengrocer is a shop selling fruit and vegetables.
fact: The dog's bowl means one dog's bowl; the dogs' bowl means one bowl shared by several dogs; the dog's bowls means one dog with several bowls.
fact: Both James's book and James' book are used; style guides differ, and what matters is being consistent.
fact: St James' Park is the football ground in Newcastle; St James's Park is a royal park in London; for names, copy the official spelling.
fact: Shops choose their own names: Sainsbury's keeps its apostrophe, Harrods has none, and Waterstones dropped its apostrophe in 2012, causing an argument in the newspapers.
fact: In 2009 Birmingham City Council dropped apostrophes from its street signs, and many people protested; in 2013 Mid Devon council tried a similar ban and changed its mind after complaints.
fact: Modern style guides write decades as "the 1990s" with no apostrophe; in "the '90s" the apostrophe shows the missing "19".
fact: It's means it is or it has; its means belonging to it and has no apostrophe, like his and her.
fact: The Apostrophe Protection Society was set up in 2001 and closed in 2019; its founder John Richards said "ignorance and laziness" had won, while others said apostrophes aren't that important.
fact: Plurals not ending in s add 's for belonging: children's, men's, women's, people's.
fact: Plurals ending in s add just an apostrophe for belonging: ladies', the girls' changing room.
exercise: A sign says "Apple's 40p, Banana's 30p." What should it say? | Apples 40p, Bananas 30p | Apples' 40p, Bananas' 30p | Apple's 40p, Bananas 30p | Apples's 40p, Bananas's 30p
exercise: What is the "greengrocer's apostrophe"? | A wrong apostrophe in a plain plural | The apostrophe in the word greengrocer's | An apostrophe used only for fruit | A special apostrophe for shop names
exercise: Oscar writes "the dogs' bowl". How many dogs share it? | More than one | Exactly one | None | It is impossible to tell
exercise: One dog has three bowls. Which is right? | the dog's bowls | the dogs' bowl | the dogs bowl's | the dog's bowl's
exercise: Kate writes "James's book", her friend "James' book". Who is right? | Both styles are used; be consistent | Only Kate | Only her friend | Neither, it should be "Jame's book"
exercise: Fin sees "St James' Park" in Newcastle and "St James's Park" in London. What should he write? | Each one's own official spelling | Always St James's | Always St James' | St Jameses Park for both
exercise: Why does Harrods have no apostrophe but Sainsbury's has one? | Shops choose their own names | Harrods broke the law | Sainsbury's made a mistake | The rules changed in 2012 for everyone
exercise: What did Waterstones do in 2012? | Dropped the apostrophe from its name | Banned apostrophes in its books | Added an apostrophe to its name | Closed all its shops
exercise: What did Birmingham City Council do in 2009? | Dropped apostrophes from street signs | Made apostrophes compulsory | Fined people for wrong apostrophes | Renamed every street
exercise: Ollie writes "The 1990's were cool." What do modern style guides write? | the 1990s | the 1990's | the 1990s' | the 19'90s
exercise: In "the '90s", what does the apostrophe show? | The missing "19" | That the 90s owned something | That it is a plural | Nothing, it is decoration
exercise: Molly writes "The cat licked it's paw." What's right? | its paw | it's paw | its' paw | it is paw
exercise: When did the Apostrophe Protection Society close? | 2019 | 2001 | 1990 | It is still open
exercise: Jack writes "The childrens' toys". What's right? | the children's toys | the childrens toys | the childrens's toys | the children toys's
exercise: Kian sees "Ladies' toilets" and "Mens toilets". Which needs fixing? | Mens should be Men's | Ladies' should be Lady's | Both are correct | Ladies' should be Ladies
exercise: How many apostrophe mistakes are in "The boy's ate there apple's and it's core's"? | Three: boy's, apple's, core's | Two: boy's and it's | One: core's | Four: boy's, apple's, it's, core's
exercise: Two girls share one tent. Which is right? | the girls' tent | the girl's tent | the girls tent's | the girls's tent
test: Your mate's market stall sign says "Fresh egg's £2." He goes "what, it's proper English, innit." What's the fix? | Fresh eggs £2 | Fresh eggs' £2 | Fresh egg's' £2 | It's fine as it is
test: A Gen Z cousin says "apostrophes are mid, nobody cares, fr." ("Mid" means meh, "fr" means for real.) What's true? | Some people care a lot; councils even got complaints about removing them | Nobody has ever argued about apostrophes | Apostrophes were banned in 2012 | Only teachers have ever noticed them
test: What would happen if we stopped using apostrophes for belonging? | "The dog's bowl" and "the dogs' bowl" would look the same | Nothing at all would change | Every plural would need one | Names would lose their capitals
test: Your brother writes "the boys' football" about his own one ball. He has no brothers. What should it be? | the boy's football | the boys' football | the boys football's | the boy's' football
test: A Newcastle fan writes "St James's Park". A Geordie mate says "nah, man, it's St James' Park, like on the sign." Who's right for that ground? | The Geordie mate, copy the official name | The fan, there's one rule for all | Both are wrong | Nobody can know
test: Grandad grumbles "Waterstones without its apostrophe, the cheek!" Did the shop break a law? | No, companies choose their own names | Yes, there's an apostrophe law | Yes, it was fined in 2012 | No, because apostrophes don't exist
test: In a cartoon, a sign says "Ladies toilet's". Fix it. | Ladies' toilets | Ladie's toilets | Ladies toilet's' | Lady's toilet's
test: A boomer uncle writes "the 1980's were rad." ("Rad" means great.) How would a modern style guide write the decade? | the 1980s | the 1980's | the 80s's | the 1980s'
test: Your mate types "The phone lost it's charge." What's the quick test? | Try "it is": "lost it is charge" fails, so its | Add more apostrophes until it looks right | Use it's whenever the word ends in s | Ask whether the phone is plural
test: A sign on a door says "Womens changing room". Fix it. | Women's changing room | Womens' changing room | Womens's changing room | Woman's' changing room
test: Puzzle: "The teachers' room" and "The teacher's room". In which does one teacher own the room? | The teacher's room | The teachers' room | Both | Neither
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the functions of the apostrophe? | It denotes the omission of letters or indicates possession | It denotes the plural number of nouns | It indicates an interrogative sentence | It separates items enumerated in a list
exam: Identify the formulation which indicates a single bowl held in common by several dogs. | the dogs' bowl | the dog's bowl | the dog's bowls | the dogs bowl's
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the possessive forms "James's" and "James'". | Both are in use, consistency within a text being the principal requirement | The former is erroneous in every circumstance | The latter is erroneous in every circumstance | Both forms are prohibited by law
exam: Ascertain the correctly punctuated form of the notice. | Men's toilets | Mens toilets | Mens' toilets | Men's' toilets
